   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I posted <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/35736896/ggplot-a-panel-of-histograms-with-histograms-as-subplots/35737909?noredirect=1#comment59175590_35737909\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/35736896/ggplot-a-panel-of-histograms-with-histograms-as-subplots/35737909?noredirect%3D1%23comment59175590_35737909&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792368188651000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1nPn9OEuSqnYoN0KQe90xZ\">this question</a> at stack overflow and got a great answer. Essentially it is about adding an inset plot to each facet created by ggplot2 with facet_grid.</div><div>The solution in the answer is to use ggplotGrob but in the answer the location of each inset is hard-coded. In my real data I&#39;m using this graphing strategy for different data sets with different numbers of facets, and therefore was wondering if there&#39;s a way to predetermine the x-location of the inset plots according to how many facets are plotted in the main plot.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a lot,<br></div><div>rubi</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9ssYI0x7CAAJ",
   "from": "Anant Gupta",
   "author": "26c1dcb4f0b4b25b",
   "date": "2016-03-03T12:25:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Adding ggplotGrob at a predefined location",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">It is not exactly what you wanted, but check whether this will do the trick<div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>plot1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_histogram</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">colour</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;black&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fill</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;white&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> facet_grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> site</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>plot2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">site_subjects</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_bar</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> facet_grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> site</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;gridExtra&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">arrange</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">arrangeGrob</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">plot1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> plot2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ncol </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Anant</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>On Thursday, March 3, 2016 at 3:50:26 AM UTC+5:30, rubi wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I posted <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/35736896/ggplot-a-panel-of-histograms-with-histograms-as-subplots/35737909?noredirect=1#comment59175590_35737909\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/35736896/ggplot-a-panel-of-histograms-with-histograms-as-subplots/35737909?noredirect%3D1%23comment59175590_35737909&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792368188653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0wc_uLOVB1IR7kU2oON1oC\">this question</a> at stack overflow and got a great answer. Essentially it is about adding an inset plot to each facet created by ggplot2 with facet_grid.</div><div>The solution in the answer is to use ggplotGrob but in the answer the location of each inset is hard-coded. In my real data I&#39;m using this graphing strategy for different data sets with different numbers of facets, and therefore was wondering if there&#39;s a way to predetermine the x-location of the inset plots according to how many facets are plotted in the main plot.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a lot,<br></div><div>rubi</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
